::GEO_U04_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size but preserves shape? {
=A. Dilation#A dilation changes size by a scale factor while preserving shape.
~B. Translation#A translation preserves both size and shape.
~C. Reflection#A reflection preserves size and shape.
~D. Rotation#A rotation preserves size and shape.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation has scale factor 2. What similarity conclusion is supported? {
~A. The image is congruent to the preimage.#Congruence would require the same size.
=B. The image is similar to the preimage.#A dilation preserves shape and changes size, so it supports similarity.
~C. The image has unrelated angles.#Dilation preserves angle measures.
~D. The image cannot be compared.#The figures can be compared through the dilation.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence says reflect, then dilate. What happens first? {
~A. Dilate#Dilate happens after reflect in this sequence.
~B. Translate#Translation is not named.
=C. Reflect#Transformation sequences are performed in the order given.
~D. Rotate#Rotation is not named.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure maps onto itself after a 180&deg; turn. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry uses a mirror line.
~B. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry uses a slide.
~C. No symmetry#The mapping onto itself shows symmetry.
=D. Rotation symmetry#A turn that maps a figure onto itself is rotation symmetry.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A line divides a figure into matching mirror halves. What symmetry is shown? {
=A. Reflection symmetry#A mirror line shows reflection symmetry.
~B. Rotation symmetry#Rotation symmetry requires a turn.
~C. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry requires a slide.
~D. Dilation symmetry#Dilation changes size and is not this symmetry.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence can justify similarity? {
~A. Translation only with scale factor 1#Translation alone proves congruence, not non-congruent similarity.
=B. Dilation followed by a translation#A dilation can change size while preserving shape, and translation is rigid.
~C. Reflection only with no size change#Reflection alone preserves size and proves congruence if it maps exactly.
~D. Rotation only with no size change#Rotation alone preserves size and proves congruence if it maps exactly.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation sequence is written in the correct order for \"rotate, then dilate\"? {
~A. Dilation followed by rotation#This reverses the sequence.
~B. Translation followed by reflection#These transformations are not named.
=C. Rotation followed by dilation#The phrase says rotate first, then dilate.
~D. Reflection followed by dilation#Reflection is not named.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A wallpaper pattern slides to match the next repeated section. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Reflection symmetry#Reflection uses a mirror line.
~B. Rotation symmetry#Rotation uses a turn.
~C. No symmetry#The repeated slide shows symmetry.
=D. Translation symmetry#A slide that maps a repeated pattern onto itself is translation symmetry.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Why does a dilation not usually prove congruence? {
=A. It changes side lengths when the scale factor is not 1.#Congruence requires same size, and non-unit dilations change lengths.
~B. It changes all angle measures.#Dilation preserves angle measures.
~C. It cannot preserve shape.#Dilation preserves shape.
~D. It is always a reflection.#Dilation is a separate transformation type.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is dilated by scale factor 1/3 and then rotated. What is true? {
~A. The image is always congruent to the original.#Scale factor 1/3 changes size, so congruence is not guaranteed.
=B. The image is similar to the original.#A dilation plus rigid motion supports similarity.
~C. The image has no corresponding angles.#Similarity preserves corresponding angles.
~D. The rotation changes the scale factor to 3.#A rotation does not change the dilation scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size but preserves shape? {
~A. Translation#A translation preserves both size and shape.
~B. Reflection#A reflection preserves size and shape.
=C. Dilation#A dilation changes size by a scale factor while preserving shape.
~D. Rotation#A rotation preserves size and shape.
}

::GEO_U04_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation has scale factor 2. What similarity conclusion is supported? {
~A. The image is congruent to the preimage.#Congruence would require the same size.
~B. The image has unrelated angles.#Dilation preserves angle measures.
~C. The image cannot be compared.#The figures can be compared through the dilation.
=D. The image is similar to the preimage.#A dilation preserves shape and changes size, so it supports similarity.
}

